## Hard invariants (do NOT violate)

* **`:domain` stays pure** (stdlib + coroutines; guard-verified). No Android / Ktor / serialization / `kotlin-reflect`. No new deps.
* The builder produces a **minimal** `AiRequest`: a single `USER` message = the user's command, a vetted
**static** system prompt, generation limits. **Nothing else is assembled** — no device, usage,
calendar, location, history, contacts, or clipboard context.
* **Positive allow-list, fail-closed:** the set of context categories the builder may emit is an explicit
declared allow-list; adding a category is a deliberate, reviewed change. Anything not allow-listed must
not reach an `AiRequest`.
* **Denylist is scanned over non-user-controlled text only** (the static system prompt, field-name
inventories, allow-list category names) — **never over the user's typed command**, which is legitimate
free text and may contain any word (e.g. "open my calendar"). Scanning user content would false-positive
and is wrong. All denylist scans must be **case-insensitive** (`ignoreCase = true`).
* **No key / raw prompt in any log / crash / analytics / `toString` of the outbound surface.** The API
key never lives in `AiRequest` or `AiError` (it is in `SecureSecretStore` / the adapter's Authorization
header — Blocks J/K). L proves the outbound domain types cannot carry a credential.
* `IntentMatcher` / `HandleUserCommandUseCase` / `feature/*` untouched. Do not touch K/M/N artifacts
(no engine, no router, no use-case, no UI). `GenerativeAiEngine` is not invoked here.

